README

This sample demonstrates the GigE Vision library. It searches for GigE Vision
cameras on the local network, opens the first camera found, loads its GenICam
description and displays its features. Then it sets the camera to Mono8 pixel
format (with a maximum image size of 640x480 pixels), starts the acquisition
and shows the received images in the upper right quarter of the screen. The
frame rate and packet statistics are logged every 5 seconds.

Connect the camera to the Ethernet port of the Raspberry Pi (directly or via a
switch). The network is configured with DHCP by default. You can change this
in kernel.cpp. If the camera is not on the same subnet (e.g. it has a
link-local address, because there is no DHCP server on a direct connection), a
temporary IP address with the host part 222 in the subnet of the Raspberry Pi
is assigned to the camera using the FORCEIP command. With a direct connection
without DHCP server, a static IP address must be configured in kernel.cpp.

The log messages are written to the screen and overlap with the image. Add the
option "logdev=ttyS1" to the file cmdline.txt on the SD card to send the log
to the serial interface instead.

Build the Circle libraries and the library in addon/gigevision first. Then
enter "make" in this directory.

Power-over-Ethernet cameras need a PoE injector or switch.
